.TH INVF-ROLLBACK 8 "2026-09" "InvariantFS" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
invf-rollback \- undo the last InvariantFS sweep from its checkpoint
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B invf-rollback
.I image
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B invf-rollback
rolls a volume back to the checkpoint taken by the last
.BR invf-sweep (8)
run (WP21). It restores the checkpoint's staged journal prefix and
decapitates the append-only inode area at the checkpoint append pointer:
everything past it - post-sweep records, tombstones, the retention
registry - vanishes wholesale. The ordinary fsck rebuild machinery then
reconciles the bitmap and L2P: the pre-sweep record versions resurrect
with their retained blocks, and the sweep's own allocations are reclaimed
as orphans. The result is bit-exact the pre-sweep state.
.PP
This is an offline tool: the volume must not be mounted. The phases are
idempotent; a killed run simply continues when re-run.
.PP
The rollback window is always the
.I last
sweep: a new sweep realizes (frees) the previous checkpoint after arming
its own. Use
.BR "invf-sweep \-\-realize"
to accept a sweep without running a new one.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
Rolled back.
.TP
1
No checkpoint: nothing to roll back.
.TP
2
Refused: a live redundancy seal would be invalidated. Free it first with
.BR "invf-sweep \-\-free\-redundant" .
.TP
3
The checkpoint descriptor or its journal staging failed verification; the
post-sweep state is untouched.
.TP
5
I/O or internal error; re-run.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR invf-sweep (8),
.BR invf-fsck (8),
.BR invarifs (7)
